  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 404 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nLong range guidance depicts a summer-like pattern prevailing on \nSunday with the Atlantic high ridge axis lingering just north of the \narea. This will keep a generally E/ESE wind regime in place and \nallow for afternoon convection to initiate along the east coast sea \nbreeze boundary. Convection will then gradually push inland and \nwest through the late afternoon and early evening hours each day.\n\nModels remain fairly consistent in showing a rather sharp gradient \nof POPs Sunday afternoon with 30-40% for the Atlantic metro areas, \nand upwards of 70% for the Gulf coast. Then, a return to a more \nwidespread wet and unsettled pattern is shown by guidance during \nthe first half of next week as a trough deepens over the E CONUS. \nModels also show an associated sfc boundary reaching the northern \nportions of the state in the Monday-Tuesday timeframe. However, \nregardless of the final outcome, guidance in general show coverage\nbecoming widespread by late Monday. Highest NBM POPs will be well\ninto the 80% range by Tuesday afternoon, and remaining around \nthat range through the end of the work week. \n\nTemperatures will remain warm during the long term with afternoon \nhighs in the U80s to L90s, along with Heat Index values in the\nlow-mid 100s each afternoon.",
